00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:09.740 align:start position:0%
August 2011 and rebel forces sweep into  the capital Tripoli creating euphoria  countrywide the four decades of  repressive and dictatorial rule by  Muammar Gaddafi were over but in a  country divided along tribal and  regional lines the celebrations were  short-lived a National Transitional  Council was established to govern after  Gaddafi's death a year later in 2012  Libyans elected a new legislative  authority called the general National  Congress the GNC was given 18 months to  oversee the transition to a permanent  democracy the deadline passed and  divisions soon reemerged new elections  were held in 2014 and the new House of  Representatives was formed but many  members of the GNC refused to recognize  the authority of the new Parliament  Libyans found themselves with two  competing authorities the House of  Representatives based in Tobruk and the  TNC based in Tripoli the east-west  divide soon developed into a full-blown  military confrontation when renegade  general Khalifa Haftar backed by the  House of Representatives launched  Operation dignity accusing the Tripoli  based government of being affiliated  with what he described as militias with  extremist agendas as fighting flared in  2015 the UN stepped in brokering a  power-sharing agreement the deal signed  in Morocco established a government of  national Accord which was endorsed by  the UN and international community as  Libya's legitimate government  but two years ago hope stand the  political divide suffered another  setback when the Mandate of the GNA  ended arch rivals faced a Suraj Prime  Minister of the Tripoli based GNA and  halifa hafta had several meetings but  failed to overcome their differences in  April after ordered his so-called  National Liberation Army to capture the  capital after making initial gains the  Warlord's forces suffered a series of  defeats in street-to-street fighting on  the outskirts of Tripoli and have  remained there since two weeks ago  afters forces backed by Egypt Saudi  Arabia Russia and the UAE launched what  they described as the final battle is  already killed and wounded hundreds of  civilians and forced nearly 140,000  people from their homes  eight years after Libyans flooded the  streets calling for democracy the  country is at war and divided like never  before  Hashim ybarra al-jazeera
 

